[
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-c-05",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-b-05",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Overview and justification",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>This course is about referencing: naming your sources so a reader can judge your work and trace every claim back to where it came from. If you are starting out at university, this might be one of the first academic skills that feels unfamiliar. It is also one of the most practical: get it right from the beginning and you save yourself a great deal of trouble later.</p><p>By the end of this course you will be able to explain why sources are named, describe what a reference needs, spot and correct an inconsistent citation, identify and explain the sources in your own work, and build the habit of logging a source the moment you choose it, so your record stays organised and traceable.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-c-10",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-b-10",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Learning outcomes",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>By the end of this course, you will be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain why academic work names its sources, and how that lets a reader judge your work and trace your claims.</li><li>Describe what a reference needs, and keep every citation in one consistent style.</li><li>Identify inconsistent citations in a piece of writing, and correct them to one consistent style.</li><li>Identify the sources in a piece of work, and explain to someone unfamiliar with it why they were selected and acknowledged.</li><li>Explain why recording a source as soon as you select it, rather than afterwards, keeps every citation traceable and organised.</li><li>Keep an organised, traceable record of your sources while you gather evidence, so each one can be found and checked again later.</li></ul>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-c-15",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-b-15",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "slot__wrapper-open__practice-connection",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "In real academic practice",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>Referencing is not an exam skill that disappears after this course. Every essay, report and dissertation you write from now on will ask you to name your sources accurately and consistently, and markers notice straight away when a reference list does not match what is cited in the text. Get the habit right now, while the stakes are low, and it becomes automatic long before it matters for a big piece of work.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-c-20",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-b-20",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Signpost",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>The course has six short lessons. First, why sources are named at all. Second, what a reference needs and why one consistent style matters. Third, spotting and fixing citations that do not match your reference list. Fourth, identifying the sources in your own work and explaining why you acknowledged them. Fifth, why a source is logged the moment you select it, not afterwards. Sixth, keeping an organised source log as you gather evidence. Work through them in order; each builds on the one before.</p>",
    "instruction": "Work through the six lessons in order.",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-c-25",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-b-25",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Course instructions",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p><strong>Start and finish with a confidence check.</strong> Before the lessons, you'll rate how confident you feel about each part of this topic. You'll rate the same statements again at the end, so you can see what has changed. Your ratings are yours: they aren't marked and no one else sees them.</p><p><strong>The activities in the lessons are for practice.</strong> Questions, examples and reflective prompts are there to help you think, and they don't count towards passing. Reflective prompts are private and optional.</p><p><strong>The test at the end is the one that counts.</strong> To complete the course you need to take the final test and score at least 70%. You can retake it.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-c-30",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-OPEN-b-30",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "How this course works",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>The examples and activities in this course use invented people, authors and sources to show how things work. Unless we say otherwise, any name, citation or reference you meet in an example is fictional.</p><p>Where a lesson draws on real work, you'll find it listed under <strong>Lesson Sources</strong> at the bottom of that page. Every real source used in the course is gathered under <strong>Course Sources</strong> at the end.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
