  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-CLOSE-c-05",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-CLOSE-b-05",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Course summary",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>You have reached the end of the course. Naming your sources, building a reference, and keeping everything in one consistent style are no longer unfamiliar: you now have a first, solid foundation in referencing. You can explain why sources are named, describe what a reference needs, spot and correct an inconsistent citation, identify and explain the sources in your own work, and keep an organised, traceable log of sources as you gather them. Look back at the confidence check to see how far this has moved.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-CLOSE-c-10",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-CLOSE-b-10",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "slot__wrapper-close__practice-connection",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "In real academic practice",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>Start using what you've learned in the very next piece of work you submit: log each source as you choose it, check your reference list matches what you've cited in the text, and keep everything in one consistent style from the first draft. You will also use this habit of logging sources as you go when you come to finding and using sources for a longer piece of work.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-CLOSE-c-15",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-introductory-W-CLOSE-b-15",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Where this goes next",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>This is the first course in the Referencing pathway. The natural next step is the intermediate course, which moves from getting referencing right to judging it: checking a paraphrase against its source, weighing whether a reference list's range reflects real research, and handling source types that do not fit a standard template. The habit of logging a source as you select it, which you have just built, is exactly what makes that judgement possible later.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
